<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.7.2: Finding Slope From Tables, Graphs, and Points</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00963</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.7.2: Finding Slope From Tables, Graphs, and Points</md:title>
    <md:uuid>069d1472-f34b-45d7-948a-ab63b5205107</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Recall that the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">slope</term> of a line is the rate of change of the linear function. On the graph, it is the steepness of the line.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_431b54f0-b83b-4912-bcc2-9bda8cb5d327" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            
            <para id="para-00002">Find the slope of the line that connects \((0,0)\) and \((3,2)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Correct! \(\frac23\)</para>
            <para id="para-00004">The correct answer is \(\frac23\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_88ced5ad-5839-4508-b36d-779d23898f6a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            
            <para id="para-00005">Find the slope of the line that connects \((4,2)\) and \((10,7)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00006">Correct! \(\frac56\)</para>
            <para id="para-00007">The correct answer is \(\frac56\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="eef828ee-e5ce-4f27-8b8d-531aae6195c2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            
            <para id="para-00008">Find the slope of the line that connects \((1,-2)\) and \((2,5) \)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00009">Correct! \(7\)</para>
            <para id="para-00010">The correct answer is \(7\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3bf173be-910d-4ae4-a7c3-4d74aa5d1398" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            
            <para id="para-00011">Find the slope of the line that connects \((-3,4)\) and \((-5,-2)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00012">Correct! \(3\)</para>
            <para id="para-00013">The correct answer is \(3\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="dbdc4f2a-7893-4e9a-ba42-55e83f7eae6e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            
            <para id="para-00014">Find the slope of the line that connects \((8, 3)\) and \((10, -9)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00015">Correct! \(-6\)</para>
            <para id="para-00016">The correct answer is \(-6\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_84a05cf3-cfc6-4368-82c0-ce1a451b3371" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00017">For numbers 6 – 9, use the graph to find the slope of the line that goes through the two points.</para>
            <para id="para-00018">
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_10_03_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00019">A and B</para>
            
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00020">Correct! \(-2\)</para>
            <para id="para-00021">The correct answer is \(-2\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_081d3633-cb17-4803-8858-d3a8ca8c952b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            
            <para id="para-00022">A and D</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00023">Correct! \(2\)</para>
            <para id="para-00024">The correct answer is \(2\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_41f98757-9a5a-4acf-84a9-ef139c4934c0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            
            <para id="para-00025">B and C</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00026">Correct! \(-1\)</para>
            <para id="para-00027">The correct answer is \(-1\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ca9ce444-7dfe-45ba-b8f3-17471cc14ebf" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            
            <para id="para-00028">C and D</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00029">Correct! \(-8\)</para>
            <para id="para-00030">The correct answer is \(-8\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_55714a1e-becd-4be8-8f38-b7000dd75e5e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            
            <para id="para-00031">Determine the slope of the equation whose values are in the given table.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
              <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                  <entry>7</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                  <entry>10</entry>
                  <entry>15</entry>
                  <entry>20</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00032">Correct! \(\frac52\)</para>
            <para id="para-00033">The correct answer is \(\frac52\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6e60b31f-878d-44e9-ba3a-80f7683af806" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            
            <para id="para-00034">Determine the slope of the equation whose values are in the given table. Simplify your answer.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00002">
              <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                  <entry>8</entry>
                  <entry>11</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>-3</entry>
                  <entry>-6</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00035">Correct! \(\frac{-3}{3}=-1\)</para>
            <para id="para-00036">The correct answer is \(\frac{-3}{3}=-1\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      (    <para id="para-00037">Find the slope of the line that goes through \((-1,5)\) and \((6,2)\).</para>)
        </note>
        <section id="sect-00003">
          <title>Additional Resources</title>
          
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Slope of a Line</title>
      <para id="para-00038">Here’s how to find the slope of a line from its graph using \(m=\frac{\mathrm{rise}}{\mathrm{run}}\).</para>
      <note id="note-00002">
       <emphasis>Finding Slope from a Graph</emphasis>
        <para id="para-00039"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Locate two points on the line whose coordinates are integers.</para>
        <para id="para-00040"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Starting with one point, sketch a right triangle, going from the first point to the second point.</para>
        <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Count the rise and the run on the legs of the triangle.</para>
        <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Take the ratio of rise to run to find the slope: \(m=\frac{\mathrm{rise}}{\mathrm{run}}\).</para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00043">
       <emphasis>Example 1</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00044">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_10_05_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00045"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Locate two points on the line whose coordinates are integers.
  \((0,5)\) and \((3,3)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Starting with one point, sketch a right triangle, going from the first point to the second point.</para>
      <para id="para-00047">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_10_05_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00048"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Count the rise and the run on the legs of the triangle.
  The rise is -2.
  The run is 3.</para>
      <para id="para-00049"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Take the ratio of rise to run to find the slope: \(m=\frac{\mathrm{rise}}{\mathrm{run}}\).</para>
      <para id="para-00050">\(m=-\frac23\)</para>
      <para id="para-00051">Here’s how to find the slope of a line when given two points on the line.</para>
      <note id="note-00003">
       <emphasis>Finding Slope from Two Points</emphasis>
        <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Label the points (\(x_1, y_1)\) and (\(x_2, y_2)\).</para>
        <para id="para-00053"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the values into the slope formula: \(m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00054"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify the slope expression.</para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00055">
       <emphasis>Example 2</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00056">Use the slope formula to find the slope of the line through the points \((-2,-3)\) and \((-7,4)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00057"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Label the points \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2, y_2)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00058">\((-2,-3)\) \((-7,4)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00059">\((x_1, y_1)\) \((x_2,y_2)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00060"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute into the slope formula.</para>
      <para id="para-00061">\(m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\frac{4-(-3)}{-7-(-2)}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00062"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify</para>
      <para id="para-00063">\(m=\frac{4-(-3)}{-7-(-2)}=\frac{4+3}{-7+2}=\frac{7}{-5}\)</para>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00005">
        <title>You Try: Finding Slope from a Graph or Two Points</title>
        <exercise id="badeb0d5-6881-4e64-95d9-556d38fcc54b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00064">Find the slope of the line using the graph, then confirm your answer using the slope formula.</para>
                  <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_10_05_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
            
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            
            <para id="para-00065">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00066">\(m = -\frac35 \)</para>
            <para id="para-00067">The correct answer is .</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <para id="para-00068"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Locate two points on the line whose coordinates are integers.</para>
      <para id="para-00069">\((0,1)\) and \((5,-2)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00070"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Starting with one point, sketch a right triangle, going from the first point to the second point.</para>
      <para id="para-00071">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_10_05_04.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00072"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Count the rise and the run on the legs of the triangle.</para>
      <para id="para-00073">The rise is 3.</para>
      <para id="para-00074">The run is -5.</para>
      <para id="para-00075"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Take the ratio of rise to run to find the slope: \(m=\frac{\mathrm{rise}}{\mathrm{run}}\).</para>
      <para id="para-00076">\(m=-\frac35\)</para>
      <para id="para-00077">Now, using the slope formula:</para>
      <para id="para-00078"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Label the points \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2,y_2)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00079">\((0, 1)\) \((5,-2)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00080">\((x_1, y_1)\) \((x_2,y_2)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00081"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute into the slope formula.</para>
      <para id="para-00082">\(m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\frac{-2-1}{5-0}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00083"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
      <para id="para-00084">\(m=\frac{-2-1}{5-0}=\frac{-3}{5}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00085">The slope of the line is \(-\frac35\).</para>
      <para id="para-00086">Here’s how to find the slope of a line, given a table of values.</para>
      <para id="para-00087">
       <emphasis>Example 3</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00088">Find the slope of the line whose table of values is shown:</para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00003">
        <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>3</entry>
            <entry>5</entry>
            <entry>7</entry>
            <entry>9</entry>
            <entry>11</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>4</entry>
            <entry>7</entry>
            <entry>10</entry>
            <entry>13</entry>
            <entry>16</entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <para id="para-00089"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Locate two points on the line whose coordinates are integers.</para>
      <para id="para-00090">\((3,4)\) \((5,7)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00091"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Label the points \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2,y_2)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00092">\((3, 4)\) \((5, 7)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00093">\((x_1, y_1)\) \((x_2,y_2)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00094"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute into the slope formula.</para>
      <para id="para-00095">\(m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\frac{7-4}{5-3}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00096"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
      <para id="para-00097">\(m=\frac{7-4}{5-3}=\frac32\)</para>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00006">
        <note class="try" id="note-00004">
    <title>Try It: Finding Slope from a Table</title>
        <exercise id="_1187649f-cac9-495b-92ce-31992caae26f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00098">Find the slope of the line whose table of values is shown:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00004">
              <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>-3</entry>
                  <entry>-1</entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                  <entry>8</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            <para id="para-00099">Correct! 1</para>
            <para id="para-00100">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00101">Here’s how to find the slope from a table of values:</para>
            <para id="para-00102"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Locate two points on the line whose coordinates are integers. - Locate two points on the line whose coordinates are integers.</para>
            <para id="para-00103">\((-3,2)\) \((-1,4)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00104"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Label the points \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2,y_2)\). - Label the points \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2,y_2)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00105">\((-3, 2)\) \((-1, 4)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00106">\((x_1, y_1)\) \((x_2,y_2)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00107"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute into the slope formula. - Substitute into the slope formula.</para>
            <para id="para-00108">\(m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\frac{4-2}{-1-(-3)}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00109"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify. - Simplify.</para>
            <para id="para-00110">\(m=\frac{4-2}{-1-(-3)}=\frac{2}{2}=1\)</para>
            <para id="para-00111">Nice try!</para>
            <para id="para-00112">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00113">Here’s how to find the slope from a table of values:</para>
            <para id="para-00114"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Locate two points on the line whose coordinates are integers. - Locate two points on the line whose coordinates are integers.</para>
            <para id="para-00115">\((-3,2)\) \((-1,4)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00116"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Label the points \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2,y_2)\). - Label the points \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2,y_2)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00117">\((-3, 2)\) \((-1, 4)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00118">\((x_1, y_1)\) \((x_2,y_2)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00119"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute into the slope formula. - Substitute into the slope formula.</para>
            <para id="para-00120">\(m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\frac{4-2}{-1-(-3)}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00121"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify. - Simplify.</para>
            <para id="para-00122">\(m=\frac{4-2}{-1-(-3)}=\frac{2}{2}=1\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise></note>
      </section>
  </section>
        </section>
        
